352:For supplementary analyses, we conducted statistical comparisons between clinical cohorts and their sex-, age-, and scanning-site-matched groups. We examined the effect of ethnicity by matching it to the sex-, age-, and scanning-sitematched groups of SZ and BD. Additionally, clinical cohorts and individuals without diagnoses were matched using an R package, MatchIt 9 , with the argument of nearest neighbour matching sex, age and scanning-site (Supplementary Table 2B2H). Furthermore, we investigated the subgroups of ASD, as defined by DSM-IV, which include Asperger's Syndrome (AS) (n=138) and individuals with Autism (n=404) (Supplementary Table 2B-2H).
536:Supplementary Figure 1. Evaluation metrics across test set and test set with clinical sets show a good fit of the model in central tendency and variance (explained variance, kurtosis) and shape of the model (MSLL and skew).
624:(A) The rank differences in the z-score maps have been adjusted for multiple comparisons using Bonferroni correction (corrected p &lt; 0.05). The scale indicates two-sided Mann-Whitney U-test rank biserial correlation (rbc) reflecting the statistical significance of the findings. (B-C) The overlap maps visually represent positive and negative extreme deviations in percentage, highlighting the regions in which the deviations occurred. The clinical groups displayed a significantly higher occurrence of extreme deviations, even in cases where the corrected group comparisons did not differ significantly.
654:(A-B) The rank differences in the z-score maps shows both uncorrected and corrected for multiple comparisons using Bonferroni correction (corrected p &lt; 0.05) in voxel-wise and lobular maps. The scale indicates two-sided Mann-Whitney U-test rank biserial correlation (rbc) reflecting the statistical significance of the findings. The uncorrected comparison show that the likelihood of the clinical groups decreases when z-score increases in more regions
704:Supplementary Figure 6. Matched case-control analyses show similar patterns to normative modelingbased full-sample analyses (Fig. 3). The figure shows uncorrected (A, C) and corrected (B, D) analyses of clinical cohorts to the matched individuals without diagnoses regarding extreme positive and negative deviations. (B) Only ASD survives the multiple comparison correction on positive deviation. (D) SZ survives multiple correction in the negative deviations along with ASD. However, the uncorrected maps (A,C) shows similar patterns to the full-sample (Figure 3). The scale indicates two-sided Mann-Whitney U-test rank biserial correlation (rbc) reflecting the statistical significance of the findings.
